    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          An electric tank water heater is a simple machine: two heating elements, two thermostats and a high-limit switch. When you lose hot water, or it runs out far too fast, one of those five parts has almost always failed, and the element is the usual suspect. Testing it takes a multimeter and a screwdriver. Replacing it is an inexpensive part and an afternoon. The rules that matter are turning the power off first and never energising an element that is not under water.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How the Upper and Lower Elements Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most residential electric tank heaters have two elements, one near the top of the tank and one near the bottom, each with its own thermostat behind a metal access panel. They screw or bolt through the tank wall so the resistance coil sits in the water. When current flows through the coil, it heats up and transfers that heat directly to the water around it. There is nothing to ignite and no flame, which is why electric tanks are so reliable and why the elements are the main wear part.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the standard wiring arrangement, the two elements never run at the same time. The upper thermostat has priority. When the top of the tank is cold, the upper thermostat sends power to the upper element only, heating the water at the top so you get usable hot water quickly. Once the top is satisfied, the upper thermostat switches power down to the lower thermostat, which then runs the lower element to heat the rest of the tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because cold water enters at the bottom through the dip tube, the lower element does most of the work in daily use. Every time someone showers, cold water replaces hot at the bottom and the lower element switches on. The upper element mainly runs after large draws that cool the top of the tank too. That is why lower elements tend to fail first, and why sediment, which settles at the bottom, is so hard on them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Typical full-size heaters use elements rated at 3,500, 4,500 or 5,500 watts at 240 volts, with 4,500 watts the most common. Small point-of-use and some compact tanks use 120-volt elements, often 1,500 or 2,000 watts. The heater's rating label lists the element wattage and voltage it was designed for, and that is the number any replacement has to match.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="symptoms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Element or Thermostat? Reading the Symptoms
          </h2>

          <p>
            The symptom points you to the part. No hot water at all usually means the upper element, the upper thermostat, the high-limit switch or the power supply. Because the upper thermostat controls the whole sequence, if the upper element burns out, the top of the tank never heats, the upper thermostat never hands off to the lower one, and the whole tank goes cold. A tripped breaker or a tripped high-limit reset produces the same symptom.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hot water that runs out much faster than it used to usually means the lower element or lower thermostat. The upper element still heats the top third or so of the tank, so you get a short burst of hot water, but the bulk of the tank stays cold. Lukewarm water throughout can point to a thermostat set too low, a failing thermostat, or, on a heater with heavy sediment, a lower element buried in mineral buildup.
          </p>

          <p>
            A breaker that trips when the heater calls for heat suggests a grounded element, one whose sheath has cracked so the coil touches water or metal, or damaged wiring. Water that suddenly gets far too hot points to a thermostat stuck closed, and that is a safety issue: turn the breaker off and investigate before you use the heater again. A heater that makes popping or rumbling noises is usually telling you about sediment rather than a failed element.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Symptom</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Most likely</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Also check</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">No hot water at all</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Upper element or upper thermostat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Breaker, high-limit reset</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hot water runs out fast</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lower element or lower thermostat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dip tube, sediment</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lukewarm water</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thermostat setting or failure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sediment on lower element</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Breaker trips on heat call</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Grounded element</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wiring, loose terminals</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water far too hot</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Thermostat stuck closed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Turn off power, call a pro</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Testing an Element With a Multimeter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start at the electrical panel. Switch off the double-pole breaker that feeds the water heater, and if it is not clearly labelled, find it before you open anything rather than guessing. Remove the upper and lower access panels, fold back the insulation and remove the plastic safety covers over the thermostats. Then confirm the power is actually off with a non-contact voltage tester and a multimeter on the AC voltage setting across the element terminals. Both should read nothing.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">POWER OFF FIRST</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">240 volts across those terminals can kill.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Turn the breaker off, then verify with a tester at the element itself. A mislabelled panel, a second feed or a disconnect that was missed are all real. Never test an element for resistance with power on, and never touch the terminals with wet hands or while standing in water.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            With the power confirmed off, disconnect one of the two wires from the element so you are measuring only the element and not the rest of the circuit. Set the multimeter to the lowest ohms range and touch one probe to each element terminal. A good element shows a steady resistance, and you can predict it from the element's wattage and voltage: resistance equals voltage squared divided by watts. A 4,500-watt, 240-volt element should read about 12.8 ohms.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Element rating</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Expected resistance (approx.)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">3,500 W at 240 V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">16.5 ohms</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">4,500 W at 240 V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12.8 ohms</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">5,500 W at 240 V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10.5 ohms</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1,500 W at 120 V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9.6 ohms</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2,000 W at 120 V</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7.2 ohms</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A reading of OL, infinity or no movement at all means the coil has burned through, and the element needs replacing. A reading close to zero indicates a short. Readings a little off the calculated value are normal, since resistance changes slightly with temperature and manufacturing tolerance, but a reading wildly different from the expected range is a failed element.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then test for a ground fault. Put one probe on an element terminal and the other on the element's metal mounting flange or a bare metal part of the tank. A good element shows no continuity at all. Any reading means the sheath has failed and current can leak to the water or tank, which can trip the breaker or create a shock hazard. Replace a grounded element even if it still heats. Test both elements, since the effort is the same and they often fail close together.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="thermostats" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Testing the Thermostats and the High-Limit Reset
          </h2>

          <p>
            If both elements test good, the thermostats or the high-limit switch are next. The upper thermostat assembly usually carries the high-limit, or ECO, switch with a red reset button. It cuts power to both elements if the water overheats, typically somewhere around 170 to 180°F depending on the maker. If it has tripped, pressing the button firmly with the power off resets it, and you may hear a click.
          </p>

          <p>
            A reset that trips once can be a fluke, often after a power surge or a long heating cycle. A reset that trips repeatedly is a symptom, not a fault in itself. The usual causes are a thermostat stuck closed, which keeps an element on until the water overheats, a grounded element that heats even when the thermostat is open, or a loose wire that is overheating at a terminal. Find the cause before resetting it again.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thermostats can be checked for continuity with the power off. With the wires disconnected, a thermostat set well above the current water temperature should show continuity across its switched terminals, because it is calling for heat. Set it well below the current temperature and the circuit should open. A thermostat that never opens or never closes is faulty. Exact terminal layouts vary by model, so follow the wiring diagram on the inside of the access panel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some technicians test thermostats live, reading voltage at the elements while the heater runs. That gives a faster answer but means working near exposed 240-volt terminals, and it is a job for someone who does it routinely. If you are not confident with live electrical testing, the cold continuity tests above, or a call to a plumber or electrician, are the safer route. Thermostats are an inexpensive part, and many people simply replace both with the elements.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Choosing the Right Replacement Element
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match the voltage and wattage to the heater's rating label, not just to the old element, because a previous owner may have installed the wrong part. A 120-volt element on a 240-volt circuit will burn out almost immediately. A 240-volt element on 120 volts produces only a quarter of its rated wattage. And installing a higher wattage than the label allows can overload the wiring and breaker, which were sized for the original rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Voltage also changes output. On a 208-volt supply, common in some apartment buildings and commercial spaces, a 4,500-watt, 240-volt element produces about 3,380 watts, because output falls with the square of voltage. That is not a fault, but it explains slow recovery, and it is why some manufacturers offer 208-volt elements for those buildings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, match the mounting. Most heaters built in recent decades use a screw-in element with a one-inch threaded base, removed with a 1-1/2 inch element socket. Older heaters may use a bolt-on flange element with a four-bolt or two-bolt plate. Check the length too; some folded or fold-back elements are shorter than straight ones of the same wattage so they fit tanks with less room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then decide on watt density. High-watt-density elements pack the full wattage into a shorter, thinner coil, so the sheath runs hotter. They are typically the cheapest option and common as factory parts. Low-watt-density elements spread the same wattage over more surface area, often by folding the element back on itself, so the surface runs cooler. Element makers and plumbers commonly recommend low-watt-density elements for hard water, because a cooler surface collects scale more slowly and is less likely to burn out under it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sheath material matters too. Copper is standard and cheapest. Stainless steel and Incoloy sheaths resist corrosion and scale better and generally last longer in aggressive or hard water, at a higher price. If your heater eats elements every couple of years, a low-density stainless or Incoloy element is usually money well spent. If it is the first failure in ten years, a standard copper element matching the label is fine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="replacing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Replacing an Element: Drain or Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            With the breaker off and power verified off, close the cold water supply valve and open a hot water tap in the house to relieve pressure. For the lower element, drain the tank completely. Sediment collects at the bottom, and draining gives you a chance to flush it, which also helps the new element last. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-drain-a-water-heater/">how to drain a water heater</a> covers hooking up the hose and clearing a clogged drain valve.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the upper element, you only need to drain the water down below the element's height, which on a typical 50-gallon tank is a portion of its volume rather than all of it. Some people replace elements without draining, by closing the cold supply and the hot tap to create a vacuum that holds most of the water in while they swap the element quickly. It can work, but it reliably spills hot water down the side of the tank and onto the wiring, so draining is the cleaner and safer approach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Disconnect the wires from the old element, noting where each goes. Fit the element socket and turn counterclockwise; old elements can be very tight, and a long breaker bar helps. Brace the tank so it does not twist on its connections. Clean the threads and the gasket seat in the tank, fit the new gasket that comes with the element, and thread the new element in by hand before tightening it snugly with the socket. Reconnect the wires firmly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Now the step that saves the new element. Close the drain valve and open the cold supply with a hot tap open somewhere in the house. Let the tank fill until water runs from the tap steadily with no sputtering air, which confirms the tank is full and the upper element is submerged. Check the element threads for leaks, replace the insulation and safety covers, refit the access panels, and only then turn the breaker back on.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never turn the power on before the tank is full.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">An element that is not surrounded by water, called dry firing, heats to failure within seconds. It is the most common reason a freshly replaced element is dead on arrival. Fill the tank and bleed the air at a hot tap first, every time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="repeat-failures" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When Elements Keep Failing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Elements can last many years, but some heaters go through them repeatedly. The causes are well known. Hard water builds scale on the element surface, which insulates it from the water so it runs hotter until the coil fails. Sediment buries the lower element in the same way. Dry firing after draining kills elements instantly. And loose terminal connections overheat and damage the element and the thermostat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fixes follow from the causes. Flush the tank once or twice a year in hard-water areas to keep sediment off the lower element. Switch to a low-watt-density element, ideally with a stainless or Incoloy sheath. Check and tighten the terminal screws when you replace parts, and look for scorched wires or melted insulation, which need fixing too. If you have a water softener, check the anode more often; our guide to <a href="/water-heater-anode-rod-replacement/">water heater anode rod replacement</a> explains why.
          </p>

          <p>
            Know when repair stops making sense. If the tank is leaking, rusting at the element openings or past the typical 10 to 15 years for an electric tank, a new element buys you a short reprieve at best. Our guides to a <a href="/water-heater-leaking/">water heater leaking</a> and <a href="/how-to-tell-the-age-of-a-water-heater/">how to tell the age of a water heater</a> help you decide whether the tank itself is worth fixing.
          </p>

          <p>
            If it is time to replace, take the chance to reconsider the type. A standard electric tank is cheap to buy and simple to fix. A heat pump water heater uses much less electricity, and our <a href="/best-heat-pump-water-heater/">best heat pump water heaters</a> guide covers when one makes sense. For a straight replacement, our <a href="/best-electric-water-heater/">best electric water heaters</a> guide compares the tanks we would buy on recovery, insulation and warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Turning the power on before the tank is full</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An element exposed to air heats to failure within seconds. After any drain, refill the tank completely and run a hot tap until it flows steadily with no air before switching the breaker back on. It is the most common reason a brand-new element is dead the first time you test it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the panel label instead of a tester</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Breaker labels are often wrong, and some homes have a separate disconnect near the heater. Turn the breaker off, then confirm zero voltage at the element terminals with a non-contact tester and a multimeter before touching anything. Two minutes of checking is cheap compared with a 240-volt shock.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the element that was in the tank</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A previous owner may have installed the wrong wattage or voltage. Match the replacement to the heater's rating label, not the old part. A higher wattage than the label allows can overload the circuit, and a 120-volt element on a 240-volt circuit fails almost immediately.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing the element without checking the thermostat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the high-limit reset keeps tripping or water ran far too hot, a thermostat stuck closed may have burned out the element. Fit a new element without fixing that and it can fail the same way. Test both thermostats, and consider replacing them along with the elements on an older heater.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing resistance with the wires still connected</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Measuring an element with both wires attached can give a misleading reading through the rest of the circuit. Disconnect at least one wire before checking ohms across the terminals, and test for a ground fault between a terminal and the tank. A grounded element can still heat, so check both tests.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if my water heater element is bad?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Turn off the power, verify it is off, disconnect one wire and measure resistance across the element terminals. A 4,500-watt, 240-volt element should read about 12.8 ohms. An open reading means it has burned out. Then test from a terminal to the tank; any continuity means the element is grounded and should be replaced even if it heats.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which element goes out first, upper or lower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually the lower element. Cold water enters at the bottom, so the lower element does most of the daily heating, and sediment settles around it. A failed lower element typically gives a short burst of hot water that runs out quickly. A failed upper element usually means no hot water at all, because the upper thermostat never passes control to the lower one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I replace a water heater element without draining the tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is possible for the upper element by closing the cold supply and creating a vacuum, but it is messy and splashes water onto wiring. Draining below the element is safer. For the lower element, drain completely, which also lets you flush sediment. Always refill fully and bleed air before restoring power.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use a higher wattage element?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the heater's rating label lists that wattage as an option. The wiring and breaker were sized for the original rating, and a larger element can overload them. Matching the label is the safe choice. For faster recovery, a larger tank or a heat pump model with a hybrid mode is usually a better answer than a bigger element.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a low watt density water heater element?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It spreads its rated wattage over a larger surface area, often by folding back on itself, so the sheath runs cooler. Element makers and plumbers commonly recommend them for hard water because scale builds more slowly on a cooler surface. They usually cost a little more than high-density elements but tend to last longer in tough water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my water heater keep tripping the breaker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Common causes are a grounded element with a cracked sheath, damaged or loose wiring, or a short in a thermostat. Test each element for a ground fault from a terminal to the tank, and inspect wiring for scorching. If you cannot find the cause, call an electrician or plumber; repeated tripping is a safety signal, not a nuisance to be reset.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do water heater elements last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no fixed number. In soft water with regular flushing, elements can last many years, sometimes the life of the tank. In hard water or with heavy sediment, failures every few years are common. Low-watt-density elements, stainless or Incoloy sheaths and annual flushing all extend element life, and a failed element alone is not a reason to replace the heater.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

