        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            An electric water heater that stops heating is usually one element or one thermostat away from working again. Kill the breaker, verify it at the terminals, test each element for resistance and for a ground fault, and use the symptoms to tell an element problem from a thermostat problem. Match any replacement to the rating label, and fill the tank completely before power goes back on.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If elements keep failing, flush the sediment and step up to a low-watt-density element. If the tank itself is old or leaking, put the money toward a replacement instead. Our <a href="/best-electric-water-heater/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best electric water heaters</a> guide is where we would start.
          </p>
